1.🔹 Scenario: Pod is Running, but users can’t access the application
Key idea: Running only means the container process started. It doesn’t mean the app is healthy,
listening on the right port, or reachable. So troubleshoot layer by layer, from inside out:
App inside Pod → Pod Ready? → Service → Endpoints → Ingress/LB → DNS → Network/Firewall

Troubleshooting steps
Step 1: Check Pod status and READY column
kubectl get pods -o wide
If it shows 0/1 Running, the readiness probe is failing, and the Pod is removed from Service endpoints. Check with:
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
Step 2: Check application logs
kubectl logs <pod-name>
Step 3: Test the app inside the Pod
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- curl localhost:<port>
4.kubectl get svc
kubectl describe svc <service-name>
Step 5: Check Endpoints (very important)
kubectl get endpoints <service-name>
kubectl get pods --show-labels
Step 6: Test the Service from inside the cluster
kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox -- wget -qO- http://<service-name>:<port>
Ingress checks:
kubectl get ingress
kubectl describe ingress <name>
kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx

Step 8: Check NetworkPolicy and DNS
kubectl get networkpolicy
kubectl exec -it <pod> -- nslookup <service-name>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system   # check CoreDNS
.

🔹 Quick flow to remember
Pod Running but not accessible
        ↓
READY 0/1?        → fix readiness probe
        ↓
curl inside Pod fails? → app/port/0.0.0.0 issue
        ↓
Endpoints empty?  → selector/label mismatch
        ↓
Service works internally but not externally?
        ↓
Ingress / NodePort / LoadBalancer / Security Group / DNS

🔹 Common root causes (quick list)
1.Readiness probe failing, so the Pod isn’t in endpoints
2.App listening on 127.0.0.1 instead of 0.0.0.0
3.targetPort doesn’t match the container port
4.Service selector doesn’t match Pod labels
5.Wrong Service type (ClusterIP used for external access)
6.Ingress misconfigured, or no Ingress controller installed
7.Security Group/firewall blocking the NodePort or LB
8.NetworkPolicy blocking traffic
9.DNS not pointing to the right LB/IP
🔹 Interview-ready answer (say it like this)
“Since the Pod is Running but users can’t reach the app, I’d troubleshoot layer by layer. 
First I’d check kubectl get pods to see if it’s actually Ready, then check the logs. 
Then I’d test the app from inside the Pod using curl localhost or kubectl port-forward to confirm the application itself works.
If it does, I’d check the Service: whether the selector matches the Pod labels, whether targetPort is correct, and whether kubectl get endpoints shows the Pod IP. 
Then I’d test the Service from another Pod. If that works, the problem is in the external layer, so I’d check the Ingress rules and controller, 
the NodePort or LoadBalancer configuration, Security Groups, and DNS.
I’d fix whichever layer fails and verify access again.”

